  "correct": "A",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Read the alternating increment",
      "body": "<p>The quantity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> when n is odd and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> when n is even. Begin with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and apply the update exactly.</p>",
      "recap": "The increment alternates in sign.",
      "pitfall": "This is not one arithmetic progression with a constant difference.",
      "pause": "Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> through <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p2-b1-l17",
          "reminder": "Use the recurrence to identify and justify a repeating block."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Justify a two-step return",
      "body": "<p>Compare a term with the term two positions later by adding the two consecutive increments. Use the index parity to decide whether the original value is recovered.</p>",
      "recap": "A repeated block needs a reason from the recurrence, not just a few initial terms.",
      "pitfall": "A few displayed terms alone are not the proof of every later term.",
      "pause": "Verify the block, calculate its sum and use it to evaluate the requested total.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p2-b1-l17",
          "reminder": "When a rule depends on the index, check that its phase repeats as well."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>The terms begin 2,1,2,1,… because the first increment is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and the next <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. More generally <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so the pair 2,1 repeats.</p><p>Each pair sums to 3. There are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow></math> pairs, giving total <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>150</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>Answer A.</strong></p>",
